+## Pitfalls
+
+1. **Over-humanizing factual text.** Scientific papers, legal documents, and technical specs need precision, not personality. Don't strip the clarity from technical writing — apply this skill selectively to prose sections, not data or citations.
+
+2. **Voice calibration from a sample that contradicts itself.** If the user's writing sample is itself AI-generated, matching it defeats the purpose. Ask: "Is this sample your own writing, or generated text?" If unsure, default to natural rather than matching.
+
+3. **Removing all structure.** Lists, headers, and formatting are not AI tells. Only remove them when they follow specific AI patterns (inline-header vertical lists with bold labels, fragmented headers that restate the heading, emojis in headings). Preserving structure while fixing voice is the goal.
+
+4. **Applying patterns too mechanically.** Some patterns only matter in context. "Highlighting" as a present participle is a problem in puffery prose but fine in a UI description that says "This widget supports highlighting." Read the full sentence before flagging.
+
+5. **Ignoring the user's explicit voice request.** If the user says "make it sound more professional," don't make it casual just because you found AI patterns. The priority is the user's intent, not your pattern-match score.
+
+6. **Losing specificity during rewrite.** AI text often includes made-up citations or vague attributions ("Industry observers argue..."). When rewriting, replace with real specifics or remove entirely. Don't invent new fake specifics to replace the old ones.
+
+7. **Destroying the user's unique tics.** If the user intentionally uses em dashes, title case, or boldface as part of their established voice, respect those choices. The skill removes mechanical overuse, not intentional style.
+
+8. **Not running the anti-AI audit pass.** The two-step audit ("What makes this obviously AI?" → "Now make it not") is critical. Skipping the audit means half the patterns get missed. Always do both passes.
+
+9. **Rewriting quotes or attributed speech.** If the text contains direct quotes from real people, don't "humanize" them — they're already human. Only edit attributed text if it's clearly AI-generated filler inside the quote.
